er month and report the gap."}, {"id": "S10", "text": "Compare peak season (15 Oct to 20 Nov) with the rest of the year per destination hub: volume, average dwell and true on-time %. Which hubs absorbed the peak badly?"}, {"id": "S6", "text": "For each zone x service type: the promised days (promised minus pickup), the median actual days to final delivery, and the true on-time %. Where is the promise itself unrealistic?"}, {"id": "S4", "text": "For each rider, the share of their logged NDR reasons that are Customer Not Available, alongside their escalation rate and their share of the network's volume. Flag riders more than 2x the network average on the first measure."}, {"id": "S8", "text": "Profit per shipment by shipper, ranked, with shipment count and share of COD. Which shippers cost the network money?"}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Shipment Lookup", "tasks": [{"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for Shipper, Destination Hub, Zone, Service Type, Delivery Status and Rider that combine with the date filters (AND logic) rather than replacing them."}, {"id": "D5", "text": "Make the shipment-results table itself filterable and sortable by any visible column, so someone can browse instead of only searching by exact AWB."}, {"id": "D1", "text": "Build a search box that takes an AWB number and shows the whole journey on one screen: pickup, hub arrival, each attempt, final status, with the promised date marked and the delay (or slack) called out, plus the billing waterfall (billed minus each cost = profit)."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "On the same screen show context: shipper, service and zone, rider and vehicle, NDR reasons, proof of delivery, escalation flag, CSAT and remarks."}, {"id": "D3", "text": "Add a date-range filter on Pickup Date and a separate date-range filter on Final Status Date, and make sure both can be applied together."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "title": "Phase 3b, Dashboard Page 2: Analytics", "tasks": [{"id": "D7", "text": "Add a monthly trend panel with the dashboard's on-time % and the true on-time % on the same chart, so the gap between them is the story."}, {"id": "D6", "text": "Build a rider scorecard showing volume, first-attempt on-time %, true on-time %, RTO % and escalation rate side by side, so 'best rider' is never reduced to one number."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "Add an NDR panel: failed-attempt reasons by payment type and by rider, with the rupee cost of failure."}, {"id": "D12", "text": "Add a weight-slab x zone profit matrix with loss-making cells highlighted and shipment counts shown."}, {"id": "D14", "text": "Add a geography panel: true on-time % and profit by state, with the best and worst five towns called out."}, {"id": "D8", "text": "Add a hub x weekday-of-arrival heatmap of average dwell or on-time %, with the outlier cells unmistakable."}, {"id": "D9", "text": "Add a promise-vs-reality panel: for each zone x service, the promised days against the actual distribution of delivery days."}, {"id": "D13", "text": "Add a peak-season panel comparing peak and non-peak per hub on volume, dwell and true on-time %."}, {"id": "D15", "text": "Make every panel on Page 2 respect a global date-range filter (Pickup Date), so the whole analytics page can be viewed for any period, not just the full year."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Add a shipper panel: volume, true on-time %, COD share and profit per shipment."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a short project write-up (any format: Word, PDF, Notion, a README) with three sections: (1) **Process Flow**: how you went from the raw export to cleaned data to SQL answers to your dashboard, including the decisions you made, especially how you resolved rider identity and treated the cut-off; (2) **Key Discoveries**: your top 3-5 findings, stated the way a network head wants to hear them, not as code output; (3) **Future Growth Recommendations**: at least 3 concrete, data-backed recommendations for what Raahi Express should do before next peak season. This is the deliverable a hiring manager would actually read, so treat it that way."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S5", "text": "How strongly do lateness and repeat attempts predict CSAT? Quantify it and say how much CSAT data you really have."}, {"id": "X-S2", "text": "Estimate how many shipments and how many points of on-time % the network would recover if the worst hub's weekday problem disappeared. Show the arithmetic."}, {"id": "X-S3", "text": "Design a COD policy (a surcharge, a minimum value, or prepaid-only in some zones) and estimate its effect on profit and RTO using the data."}]}], "premium": [{"id": "X3", "text": "**Build and defend a rider scorecard.** Combine volume, first-attempt success, true on-time %, RTO rate and escalations into one weighted score. Run a sensitivity analysis showing how the top 10 changes as you vary each weight by +/-10%, and say which weighting you would recommend."}, {"id": "X2", "text": "**Prove the weekday gap is real.** One hub shows a dramatic on-time drop for shipments arriving on certain weekdays. Run a statistical test (two-proportion z-test or logistic regression) and show it survives controlling for service type, zone and peak season."}, {"id": "X1", "text": "**Normalize it.** Design a 3NF relational schema (separate tables for riders, hubs, shippers and shipments at minimum) from the flat file, load it into a real SQL database, and rewrite your S3 and S10 answers as proper JOIN queries."}, {"id": "X4", "text": "**Forecast next quarter.** Using the 12 months of history, forecast Q1 FY26-27 (Apr-Jun 2026) shipments per hub and flag which hubs will hit capacity problems, treating the peak season sensibly."}, {"id": "X5", "text": "**Set the promise scientifically.** For each zone x service, use the empirical distribution of delivery days to choose the promised days that hit a target on-time rate, and quantify the trade-off between a tighter promise and a higher breach rate."}]}$pack$::jsonb
