ute: Delivered shipments with a final status date on or before the promised date, as a share of all shipments that reached a final status (exclude In Transit). Show it next to S1 per month and report the gap."}, {"id": "S4", "text": "For each rider, the share of their logged NDR reasons that are Customer Not Available, alongside their escalation rate and their share of the network's volume. Flag riders more than 2x the network average on the first measure."}, {"id": "S1", "text": "Reproduce the Ops dashboard's on-time figure: the share of shipments whose FIRST ATTEMPT date is on or before the promised date (among shipments that had an attempt), per month and overall."}, {"id": "S8", "text": "Profit per shipment by shipper, ranked, with shipment count and share of COD. Which shippers cost the network money?"}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Shipment Lookup", "tasks": [{"id": "D1", "text": "Build a search box that takes an AWB number and shows the whole journey on one screen: pickup, hub arrival, each attempt, final status, with the promised date marked and the delay (or slack) called out, plus the billing waterfall (billed minus each cost = profit)."}, {"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for Shipper, Destination Hub, Zone, Service Type, Delivery Status and Rider that combine with the date filters (AND logic) rather than replacing them."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "On the same screen show context: shipper, service and zone, rider and vehicle, NDR reasons, proof of delivery, escalation flag, CSAT and remarks."}, {"id": "D3", "text": "Add a date-range filter on Pickup Date and a separate date-range filter on Final Status Date, and make sure both can be applied together."}, {"id": "D5", "text": "Make the shipment-results table itself filterable and sortable by any visible column, so someone can browse instead of only searching by exact AWB."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "title": "Phase 3b, Dashboard Page 2: Analytics", "tasks": [{"id": "D14", "text": "Add a geography panel: true on-time % and profit by state, with the best and worst five towns called out."}, {"id": "D15", "text": "Make every panel on Page 2 respect a global date-range filter (Pickup Date), so the whole analytics page can be viewed for any period, not just the full year."}, {"id": "D12", "text": "Add a weight-slab x zone profit matrix with loss-making cells highlighted and shipment counts shown."}, {"id": "D8", "text": "Add a hub x weekday-of-arrival heatmap of average dwell or on-time %, with the outlier cells unmistakable."}, {"id": "D9", "text": "Add a promise-vs-reality panel: for each zone x service, the promised days against the actual distribution of delivery days."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Add a shipper panel: volume, true on-time %, COD share and profit per shipment."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "Add an NDR panel: failed-attempt reasons by payment type and by rider, with the rupee cost of failure."}, {"id": "D6", "text": "Build a rider scorecard showing volume, first-attempt on-time %, true on-time %, RTO % and escalation rate side by side, so 'best rider' is never reduced to one number."}, {"id": "D13", "text": "Add a peak-season panel comparing peak and non-peak per hub on volume, dwell and true on-time %."}, {"id": "D7", "text": "Add a monthly trend panel with the dashboard's on-time % and the true on-time % on the same chart, so the gap between them is the story."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a short project write-up (any format: Word, PDF, Notion, a README) with three sections: (1) **Process Flow**: how you went from the raw export to cleaned data to SQL answers to your dashboard, including the decisions you made, especially how you resolved rider identity and treated the cut-off; (2) **Key Discoveries**: your top 3-5 findings, stated the way a network head wants to hear them, not as code output; (3) **Future Growth Recommendations**: at least 3 concrete, data-backed recommendations for what Raahi Express should do before next peak season. This is the deliverable a hiring manager would actually read, so treat it that way."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S4", "text": "The riders are paid per attempt. Propose an incentive scheme that pays for successful deliveries and estimate who would win and lose."}, {"id": "X-S5", "text": "How strongly do lateness and repeat attempts predict CSAT? Quantify it and say how much CSAT data you really have."}, {"id": "X-S1", "text": "Propose a new promised-days table per zone x service that would hit about 90% true on-time, and say what it does to the competitiveness of Zone D offers."}]}], "premium": [{"id": "X5", "text": "**Set the promise scientifically.** For each zone x service, use the empirical distribution of delivery days to choose the promised days that hit a target on-time rate, and quantify the trade-off between a tighter promise and a higher breach rate."}, {"id": "X2", "text": "**Prove the weekday gap is real.** One hub shows a dramatic on-time drop for shipments arriving on certain weekdays. Run a statistical test (two-proportion z-test or logistic regression) and show it survives controlling for service type, zone and peak season."}, {"id": "X1", "text": "**Normalize it.** Design a 3NF relational schema (separate tables for riders, hubs, shippers and shipments at minimum) from the flat file, load it into a real SQL database, and rewrite your S3 and S10 answers as proper JOIN queries."}, {"id": "X4", "text": "**Forecast next quarter.** Using the 12 months of history, forecast Q1 FY26-27 (Apr-Jun 2026) shipments per hub and flag which hubs will hit capacity problems, treating the peak season sensibly."}, {"id": "X3", "text": "**Build and defend a rider scorecard.** Combine volume, first-attempt success, true on-time %, RTO rate and escalations into one weighted score. Run a sensitivity analysis showing how the top 10 changes as you vary each weight by +/-10%, and say which weighting you would recommend."}]}$pack$::jsonb
