"S3", "text": "Using window functions, rank all 48 riders by volume, by first-attempt on-time % and by true on-time %. Return the riders in the top 5 on first-attempt on-time but in the bottom 15 on true on-time."}, {"id": "S9", "text": "Build a weight slab (0-0.5, 0.5-1, 1-2, 2-5, 5+ kg) x zone x service profit table and return the loss-making cells with at least 100 shipments."}, {"id": "S4", "text": "For each rider, the share of their logged NDR reasons that are Customer Not Available, alongside their escalation rate and their share of the network's volume. Flag riders more than 2x the network average on the first measure."}, {"id": "S12", "text": "Using a CTE, build a per-shipper funnel: picked up -> reached a first attempt -> delivered on the first attempt -> delivered eventually -> delivered on time."}]}, {"key": "dash1", "title": "Phase 3a, Dashboard Page 1: Shipment Lookup", "tasks": [{"id": "D4", "text": "Add filters for Shipper, Destination Hub, Zone, Service Type, Delivery Status and Rider that combine with the date filters (AND logic) rather than replacing them."}, {"id": "D2", "text": "On the same screen show context: shipper, service and zone, rider and vehicle, NDR reasons, proof of delivery, escalation flag, CSAT and remarks."}, {"id": "D3", "text": "Add a date-range filter on Pickup Date and a separate date-range filter on Final Status Date, and make sure both can be applied together."}, {"id": "D5", "text": "Make the shipment-results table itself filterable and sortable by any visible column, so someone can browse instead of only searching by exact AWB."}, {"id": "D1", "text": "Build a search box that takes an AWB number and shows the whole journey on one screen: pickup, hub arrival, each attempt, final status, with the promised date marked and the delay (or slack) called out, plus the billing waterfall (billed minus each cost = profit)."}]}, {"key": "dash2", "title": "Phase 3b, Dashboard Page 2: Analytics", "tasks": [{"id": "D14", "text": "Add a geography panel: true on-time % and profit by state, with the best and worst five towns called out."}, {"id": "D13", "text": "Add a peak-season panel comparing peak and non-peak per hub on volume, dwell and true on-time %."}, {"id": "D15", "text": "Make every panel on Page 2 respect a global date-range filter (Pickup Date), so the whole analytics page can be viewed for any period, not just the full year."}, {"id": "D6", "text": "Build a rider scorecard showing volume, first-attempt on-time %, true on-time %, RTO % and escalation rate side by side, so 'best rider' is never reduced to one number."}, {"id": "D9", "text": "Add a promise-vs-reality panel: for each zone x service, the promised days against the actual distribution of delivery days."}, {"id": "D8", "text": "Add a hub x weekday-of-arrival heatmap of average dwell or on-time %, with the outlier cells unmistakable."}, {"id": "D7", "text": "Add a monthly trend panel with the dashboard's on-time % and the true on-time % on the same chart, so the gap between them is the story."}, {"id": "D12", "text": "Add a weight-slab x zone profit matrix with loss-making cells highlighted and shipment counts shown."}, {"id": "D10", "text": "Add an NDR panel: failed-attempt reasons by payment type and by rider, with the rupee cost of failure."}, {"id": "D11", "text": "Add a shipper panel: volume, true on-time %, COD share and profit per shipment."}]}, {"key": "documentation", "title": "Phase 4, Documentation", "tasks": [{"id": "DOC1", "text": "Write a short project write-up (any format: Word, PDF, Notion, a README) with three sections: (1) **Process Flow**: how you went from the raw export to cleaned data to SQL answers to your dashboard, including the decisions you made, especially how you resolved rider identity and treated the cut-off; (2) **Key Discoveries**: your top 3-5 findings, stated the way a network head wants to hear them, not as code output; (3) **Future Growth Recommendations**: at least 3 concrete, data-backed recommendations for what Raahi Express should do before next peak season. This is the deliverable a hiring manager would actually read, so treat it that way."}]}, {"key": "stretch", "title": "Stretch ideas (optional)", "tasks": [{"id": "X-S2", "text": "Estimate how many shipments and how many points of on-time % the network would recover if the worst hub's weekday problem disappeared. Show the arithmetic."}, {"id": "X-S6", "text": "Analyse Lost and Damaged shipments: claim cost by hub, shipper and declared value, and whether any pattern suggests a fixable cause."}, {"id": "X-S1", "text": "Propose a new promised-days table per zone x service that would hit about 90% true on-time, and say what it does to the competitiveness of Zone D offers."}]}], "premium": [{"id": "X1", "text": "**Normalize it.** Design a 3NF relational schema (separate tables for riders, hubs, shippers and shipments at minimum) from the flat file, load it into a real SQL database, and rewrite your S3 and S10 answers as proper JOIN queries."}, {"id": "X5", "text": "**Set the promise scientifically.** For each zone x service, use the empirical distribution of delivery days to choose the promised days that hit a target on-time rate, and quantify the trade-off between a tighter promise and a higher breach rate."}, {"id": "X4", "text": "**Forecast next quarter.** Using the 12 months of history, forecast Q1 FY26-27 (Apr-Jun 2026) shipments per hub and flag which hubs will hit capacity problems, treating the peak season sensibly."}, {"id": "X2", "text": "**Prove the weekday gap is real.** One hub shows a dramatic on-time drop for shipments arriving on certain weekdays. Run a statistical test (two-proportion z-test or logistic regression) and show it survives controlling for service type, zone and peak season."}, {"id": "X3", "text": "**Build and defend a rider scorecard.** Combine volume, first-attempt success, true on-time %, RTO rate and escalations into one weighted score. Run a sensitivity analysis showing how the top 10 changes as you vary each weight by +/-10%, and say which weighting you would recommend."}]}$pack$::jsonb
